                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum.</hi></head>
                <p>The memorandum of the German legation, of May 10, 1893, on the subject of the
                    allotment of the customs revenues of Samoa, has been considered, in the light of
                    the suggestions contained in the report of the German consul at Apia, dated
                    January 27, 1893. Suggestions in a similar sense have been received from the
                    acting consul-general of the United States, under date of January 28, 1893.</p>
                <p>Conformably with the agreement of the three powers upon the general propositions
                    stated in the Department’s note of November 22, 1892, and accepting the
                    suggestions of Dr. von Holleben’s present memorandum, this Government will
                    forthwith send, by way af Auckland, a telegraphic instruction to the chief
                    justice of Samoa that his opinion touching the allotment of the customs revenues
                    is not accepted, and that he will concert with the consular representatives of
                    the three powers to effect the understanding reached by them in this regard as
                    reported in January last.</p>
                <p>A confirmatory telegram in the same sense will be dispatched to Acting
                    Consul-General Blacklock.</p>
                <closer><dateline rendition="#float-left">Department of State,
                            <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1893-05-12">May 12, 1893</date>.</dateline></closer>
            </div>
